  "answer": "The test imports `sumEntries` from `src/index.ts`, but the helper is defined as `computeTotal` in `src/core.ts` and only used internally by the `total` wrapper. I'll rename the definition and the internal use, then re-export the helper from the index.\nBoth edits applied. Now let me confirm no stray `computeTotal` references remain and run the test:\nDone — the test passes.\n\n## What changed\n\n**`src/core.ts`** (renamed definition)\n- `export function computeTotal(values: number[])` → `export function sumEntries(values: number[])`. The reduce-based summing logic is untouched.\n\n**`src/index.ts`** (renamed internal use + added export)\n- Import changed from `computeTotal` to `sumEntries`.\n- Added `export { sumEntries }` so the helper is exported from the package entry point under its new name (this is what makes the existing test import resolve).\n- The `total` wrapper is behaviorally identical — it now delegates to `sumEntries(values)` instead of `computeTotal(values)`.\n\n**`test/oracle.test.ts`** — unchanged, as required. It still imports `{ total, sumEntries }` from `../src/index`.\n\nA workspace-wide grep confirms no references to `computeTotal` remain. `bun test` result: 1 pass, 0 fail (both assertions — `sumEntries([1, 2, 3]) === 6` and `total([4, 5]) === 9` — succeeded). AGENTS.md, package.json, and the test file were left as-is."
